From: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
Date: Fri, 09 Oct 2026 17:29:20 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] hostapd: classify a DFS event by the kernel background flag

hostapd classifies a DFS event as an event of its background chain
by the primary frequency of its chain record. The record can outlive
the chain, because the kernel ends an idle chain without an event
when the AP switches onto its block. A CAC that the AP then runs on
that primary counts as the chain CAC. Its abort leaves the switch
state set, and a later radar does not move the AP. The kernel also
aborts a chain when the AP starts a CAC inside the chain block.
hostapd can read that abort after it armed a replacement chain.

Linux commit 68b908b3c83b ("wifi: cfg80211: events, report background
radar") adds a background flag to a DFS event whose channel matches
the kernel's chain. hostapd classifies an event by that flag alone,
because the kernel in this tree sends it. A flagged event without a
record sets its record from the event, so that a chain that the kernel
runs stays usable. An event on the AP block sets no record, because
the AP can move onto the block of a running chain; when that chain's
CAC completes, hostapd arms a new chain in another block. A flagged
radar on the AP block moves the AP, because the chain can run there
after a restart drops its record. A flagged abort arms a new chain
only when no record exists or its frequency lies in the recorded
block. The abort of a replaced chain then leaves the replacement in
place. A flagged radar outside the recorded block marks its channels
unavailable and moves nothing. The firmware can report one radar
twice, and a search for the second report finds no free block and
drops the record of a running chain.

Signed-off-by: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
Co-developed-by: Piotr Kubik <piotr.kubik@adtran.com>
Signed-off-by: Piotr Kubik <piotr.kubik@adtran.com>
